preConditionHook:
// The first close through the average: the previous close was at or below
// the EMA and this close is above it (short: the mirror). Brooks's 'the
// first close below the moving average' after a long run on the other side.
//
// WRITTEN AS THE TWO-BAR EVENT TEST, NOT EL'S `crosses above`, and the
// choice is the rule's specification, not a translation detail:
//   * the merged pseudocode is the two-bar test, and the merge notes
//     contrast it with MovingAverageCross precisely because that rule goes
//     through EL's bridging state machine and this one does not;
//   * `crosses above` is VERIFIED as a STATE MACHINE -- it fires against
//     the most recent NON-equal side, which can be many bars back, and it
//     BRIDGES an equality run so above -> equal -> above does not re-fire.
//     That is a different set of bars, i.e. a different rule;
//   * the register's REFUTATION of el_gt(A,B) && el_le(A[1],B[1]) is a
//     refutation of that expression AS A MIRROR OF THE WORD `crosses`.
//     Neither twin uses the word, so nothing is being mirrored: both sides
//     spell the two-bar test out and agree bar for bar;
//   * the lagged read this needs was MEASURED on 2026-09-17, which is why
//     the rule was held back at all -- the crossing formulation would never
//     have needed it.
// Consequence, stated rather than hidden: an equality run re-fires here
// where `crosses` would bridge it. With an EMA against a close that costs
// bars only inside EL's 2.22e-12 relational tolerance.
//
// Not CloseBeyondEma, which is the STATE (close beyond the average on this
// bar) where this is the EVENT -- the relation MomentumChange has to
// Momentum. Never split: a crossing is inherently two-bar.
//
// LAGGED EMA READS, MEASURED 2026-09-17 (EL_XAverage_Lag_Probe.txt): the
// function's own [k] history is bit for bit the value it printed k bars
// earlier -- the lag1 and lag5 columns were exactly 0 on every bar past the
// lag on both charts (9,664 + 8,157 and 9,659 + 8,152 bars) -- and BEFORE
// that history exists ([k] on bars 1..k) the read returns 0.0, not the seed
// and not a -1 sentinel. So the zero-initialized mirrored ring buffer below
// is EXACT, and the rule GUARDS its own first k bars on the bar counter
// instead of comparing against that zero.
emaPrev = emaVal;
// MultiWalk's WFSafe_Xaverage, the function the EasyLanguage twin calls,
// ported unchanged from EmaGapBar (the EMA reference, which carries the full
// measurements). MEASURED, EL_XAverage_Probe.txt (@OJ 240min, chart starts
// 2007 and 2010):
//   * the wrapper is BIT-IDENTICAL to the plain XAverage built-in at a fixed
//     length -- (wf - xa) * 1e12 printed exactly 0.0000 on all 17,785 bars;
//   * the SEED is the PRICE on the study's first calculated bar
//     (ctx.CurrentBar() == 1), not a length-term simple average;
//   * the recurrence is x + sf*(p - x), ONE statement, never sf*p + (1-sf)*x:
//     the two separated on 67% of probed bars past 100. Do not re-associate;
//   * there is NO warm-up hole, so no bar-count guard returning 0 on the
//     CURRENT value;
//   * an EMA NEVER FORGETS ITS SEED -- it only decays at (1 - sf)^n, ~330
//     bars -- so Max Bars Back and the chart start are part of this rule's
//     specification.
// LENGTH CHANGE MEASURED 2026-09-17 (EL_XAverage_Lag_Probe.txt): the wrapper
// recomputes sf on the bar the length changes and carries its state, with no
// re-seed (reconstruction error 3.6e-15; the three rival hypotheses were off
// by 1.9 to 42 price units). emaLength MAY therefore be optimized in grids --
// the "pin emaLength" caveat is retired, and this mirror, which derives sf
// from the raw input every bar and carries emaVal, already behaves that way.
// Scratch below is declared in localVariables, not at hook scope: the
// generator only prefixes declared locals, so a hook-scope declaration is a
// redefinition when this rule is placed twice in one strategy.
emaSf = 2.0 / (emaLength + 1.0);
if (ctx.CurrentBar() == 1) {
    emaVal = close[0];
} else {
    emaVal = emaVal + emaSf * (close[0] - emaVal);
}
// Guard the one bar on which emaPrev is the pre-history zero. Without it
// the short side would fire spuriously on bar 2 of a positively priced
// series, where close[1] >= 0.0 always holds.
crossReady = ctx.CurrentBar() > 1;
